data/evidence/synthesis-proposals.md:115:Promoted 2026-05-16. Anchor sources: Hands 2024 (US cocoa multi-year n=72, Welch T-test organic Cd statistically significantly higher), Brum 2025 (European flaxseed n=52 ICP-OES, organic Pb mean 652 µg/kg vs conventional 249 µg/kg, p<0.05), Potortì 2026 (Italian-market tahini n=7, sole EU-exceedance outlier T-1 was Turkey-origin organic, Cd 0.35 mg/kg = 3.5× limit and Pb 0.50 mg/kg = 5× fats/oils limit), Słepecka 2017 (Polish cereal products, ecological bran Cd 26.3 mg/kg vs undetectable conventional; ecological flour Cd 12.28 mg/kg), Gardener 2019 (US baby foods Clean Label Project n=564, Wilcoxon p=0.65 Cd / p=0.28 Pb — organic null), Grochowska-Niedworok 2020 (Polish tomatoes, organic higher dry-mass Cd than conventional), Dearing 2025 (NZ vegetables n=153 composites post-Cyclone-Gabrielle, counterexample: organic LOWER Cd p=0.003 and Ni p<0.001). Seven datasets across four continents, six matrices (cocoa, flaxseed, sesame paste, cereal/bran, infant formula and baby food, tomatoes; NZ mixed vegetables as the bounded counterexample). Mechanism rests on input-vs-occurrence orthogonality of USDA NOP and EU 2018/848, single-origin organic sourcing selecting for high-contamination soil regions, manure and natural-phosphate input streams higher in Cd than EU-Cd-limited synthetic NPK, and lower-pH organic farm soils increasing Cd bioavailability. Load-bearing for brand-legal defense, consumer communication, and articulating the complementary-versus-substitutable relationship between organic certification and HMTc. Synthesis page: [[../../wiki/synthesis/organic-certification-not-protective|organic-certification-not-protective]].
